# The ZECKENDORF REPRESENTATION, machine-checked. Zeckendorf's theorem says every positive integer is a sum of distinct
# Fibonacci numbers in EXACTLY ONE way that uses NO TWO CONSECUTIVE Fibonacci numbers. Written as a string of bits, one
# per consecutive Fibonacci number, the representation is a binary-looking word with NO TWO ADJACENT ONES. That word is
# the regular-language condition behind Margenstern's FIBONACCI ADDRESSING of the hyperbolic tilings: the tiles of a
# tessellation are numbered row by row, each number is written in this non-adjacent form, and the resulting bit string IS
# the tile's coordinate. It encodes the path down the spanning tree to that tile, and the set of all valid addresses is a
# regular language, which is why a small finite automaton can navigate an infinite curved mesh.
#
# This file models the bit string as an inductive type `bits` (a list read low-Fibonacci-first: the first bit is the
# coefficient of F2 = 1, the next of F3 = 2, then F4 = 3, F5 = 5, ...). `is-valid` scans the string and answers whether
# it satisfies the non-adjacent-ones condition. `value` reads the string back as the natural number it stands for, the
# sum of the Fibonacci numbers at the on-bit positions. The kernel then checks, by computing normal forms (`calm hold`)
# and by structural induction (`fold`), that the validity scanner accepts sparse codes and rejects adjacent ones, that
# the predicate is closed under the recursion, that small codes read back to the right numbers, and that two different
# valid codes carry different values. The full uniqueness theorem over all naturals is stated but not discharged here:
# what is proven is marked plainly below, and nothing is claimed that the kernel did not check.

# A natural number: zero, or the successor of a natural.
form natural
  case zero
  case succ
    link prior, like natural

# A truth value used as the result of the validity scan and the equality test.
form flag
  case yes
  case no

# Addition by recursion on the first argument: 0 + b = b, and (succ a) + b = succ (a + b). Needed to total the Fibonacci
# numbers at the on-bit positions. Recursion descends on the matched predecessor, so it is verified terminating and the
# kernel unfolds it.
task plus
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      send back
        read b
    case succ
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call plus
              read prior
              read b

# The Fibonacci function by two-step recursion: fib 0 = 0, fib 1 = 1, fib (n+2) = fib (n+1) + fib n. The on-bit at index
# i contributes fib i to the value.
task fib
  take n, like natural
  like natural
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        make zero
    case succ
      link m
      fork case, read m
        case zero
          send back
            make succ
              bind prior
                make zero
        case succ
          link k
          send back
            call plus
              call fib
                read m
              call fib
                read k

# Equality of two naturals by simultaneous recursion: yes when both reach zero together, no the moment they disagree.
# Used to witness that two codes carry different values.
task is-equal-natural
  take a, like natural
  take b, like natural
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case zero
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make yes
        case succ
          send back
            make no
    case succ
      link pa
      fork case, read b
        case zero
          send back
            make no
        case succ
          link pb
          send back
            call is-equal-natural
              read pa
              read pb

# A Fibonacci-bit string, read low-Fibonacci-first. `done` is the end of the string. `zero rest` is an off bit (that
# Fibonacci number is not used) followed by the rest. `one rest` is an on bit (that Fibonacci number is used) followed by
# the rest. The first bit is the coefficient of F2, the next of F3, and so on up the Fibonacci sequence.
form bits
  case done
  case zero
    link rest, like bits
  case one
    link rest, like bits

# The validity scan, carrying a flag for whether the PREVIOUS bit was on. Reaching `done` is valid. An off bit clears the
# previous-on flag and continues. An on bit is a violation exactly when the previous bit was also on (two adjacent ones),
# otherwise it sets the flag and continues. This is the finite automaton that recognizes the regular language of
# Margenstern's tile addresses.
task valid-from
  take prev, like flag
  take xs, like bits
  like flag
  fork case, read xs
    case done
      send back
        make yes
    case zero
      send back
        call valid-from
          make no
          read rest
    case one
      fork case, read prev
        case yes
          send back
            make no
        case no
          send back
            call valid-from
              make yes
              read rest

# A code is valid when it satisfies the non-adjacent-ones condition, scanned from the start with no previous on bit.
task is-valid
  take xs, like bits
  like flag
  send back
    call valid-from
      make no
      read xs

# The natural number a code stands for, read from a given starting Fibonacci index. `done` contributes nothing. An off
# bit advances the index. An on bit adds fib at the current index, then advances. This is the sum of the Fibonacci
# numbers at the on-bit positions.
task value-from
  take index, like natural
  take xs, like bits
  like natural
  fork case, read xs
    case done
      send back
        make zero
    case zero
      send back
        call value-from
          make succ
            bind prior
              read index
          read rest
    case one
      send back
        call plus
          call fib
            read index
          call value-from
            make succ
              bind prior
                read index
            read rest

# The value of a whole code, starting from Fibonacci index 2 (the first bit is the coefficient of F2 = 1).
task value
  take xs, like bits
  like natural
  send back
    call value-from
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make zero
      read xs

# An all-zeros code of n bits, the zero-padded empty string. Used to prove padding never changes the on-bit count.
task zeros
  take n, like natural
  like bits
  fork case, read n
    case zero
      send back
        make done
    case succ
      send back
        make zero
          bind rest
            call zeros
              read prior

# The number of on bits in a code, by recursion on its structure.
task count-on
  take xs, like bits
  like natural
  fork case, read xs
    case done
      send back
        make zero
    case zero
      send back
        call count-on
          read rest
    case one
      send back
        make succ
          bind prior
            call count-on
              read rest

# THE EMPTY CODE IS VALID: the string with no bits satisfies the condition. The base case of the scan.
rule done-is-valid
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-valid
        make done
      make yes
  calm hold

# ACCEPTS A SPARSE CODE: the code 1, 0, 1 (ones separated by an off bit) has no two adjacent ones, so the scanner accepts
# it. This is a valid Fibonacci address.
rule accepts-sparse-code
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-valid
        make one
          bind rest
            make zero
              bind rest
                make one
                  bind rest
                    make done
      make yes
  calm hold

# REJECTS TWO ADJACENT ONES: the code 1, 1 uses two consecutive Fibonacci numbers, which Zeckendorf's condition forbids,
# so the scanner rejects it. This is the violation the regular language excludes.
rule rejects-adjacent-ones
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-valid
        make one
          bind rest
            make one
              bind rest
                make done
      make no
  calm hold

# CLOSED UNDER A LEADING ZERO: a code is valid exactly when the same code with an off bit prepended is valid, for EVERY
# code. Prepending an off bit cannot create a new adjacency, so validity is preserved. (The off-bit branch resets the
# previous-on flag, the same state the scan starts in, so both sides reduce to the same scan.)
rule is-valid-closed-under-leading-zero
  seat xs, like bits
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-valid
        make zero
          bind rest
            read xs
      call is-valid
        read xs
  calm hold

# A LEADING ON BIT AFTER AN ON BIT ALWAYS REJECTS: once the previous bit is on, an on bit fails the scan no matter what
# follows, for EVERY tail. This is the heart of the non-adjacency rule.
rule on-after-on-always-rejects
  seat xs, like bits
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call valid-from
        make yes
        make one
          bind rest
            read xs
      make no
  calm hold

# THE CODE 1 READS AS ONE: the single on bit at the first position contributes F2 = 1, so its value is 1.
rule value-of-one-is-one
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call value
        make one
          bind rest
            make done
      make succ
        bind prior
          make zero
  calm hold

# THE CODE 0, 1 READS AS TWO: the on bit at the second position contributes F3 = 2, so its value is 2.
rule value-of-zero-one-is-two
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call value
        make zero
          bind rest
            make one
              bind rest
                make done
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make zero
  calm hold

# THE CODE 1, 0, 1 READS AS FOUR: on bits at positions one and three contribute F2 + F4 = 1 + 3 = 4. This ties the bit
# string back to the number it addresses.
rule value-of-one-zero-one-is-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call value
        make one
          bind rest
            make zero
              bind rest
                make one
                  bind rest
                    make done
      make succ
        bind prior
          make succ
            bind prior
              make succ
                bind prior
                  make succ
                    bind prior
                      make zero
  calm hold

# INJECTIVITY WITNESS: the code 1 (value 1) and the code 0, 1 (value 2) are two DIFFERENT valid codes carrying DIFFERENT
# values, so the value reading is not constant. The equality test on their values answers no. This is a concrete instance
# of the injectivity half of Zeckendorf's theorem.
rule different-codes-carry-different-values
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-equal-natural
        call value
          make one
            bind rest
              make done
        call value
          make zero
            bind rest
              make one
                bind rest
                  make done
      make no
  calm hold

# PADDING WITH ZEROS NEVER ADDS AN ON BIT: an all-zeros code of any length has no on bits, for EVERY length n. Proved by
# induction on n: the empty code has none, and adding one more off bit adds none. This is why trailing or leading zero
# padding leaves a Fibonacci address unchanged.
rule zeros-have-no-on-bits
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call count-on
        call zeros
          read n
      make zero
  fold n

# PADDING WITH ZEROS STAYS VALID: an all-zeros code of any length is valid, for EVERY length n. Proved by induction on n:
# the empty code is valid, and prepending an off bit preserves validity. The vacuous address (no Fibonacci numbers used)
# is always a legal coordinate.
rule zeros-are-valid
  seat n, like natural
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call is-valid
        call zeros
          read n
      make yes
  fold n
    cite is-valid-closed-under-leading-zero

# WHAT IS PROVEN VERSUS STATED. Proven above by the kernel: the scanner accepts a sparse code and rejects two adjacent
# ones, the validity predicate is closed under a leading off bit and always rejects an on bit following an on bit, the
# small codes read back to their exact numbers, two different valid codes carry different values (an injectivity
# witness), and zero padding preserves both the on-bit count and validity (two genuine inductions). NOT proven here, only
# stated: the full Zeckendorf theorem that EVERY positive natural has EXACTLY ONE valid code. That existence-and-
# uniqueness statement quantifies over all naturals and needs the greedy construction together with a strong-induction
# bound on partial Fibonacci sums, which is beyond the discharging done in this file. The fragments above are the honest
# checked core of it.
